<div class="hr-page">

  <header class="hr-header">
    <div class="hr-eyebrow">The Power Law &middot; the network</div>
    <h1>The hash rate, as a <em>power law</em></h1>
    <p class="hr-subtitle">Bitcoin&rsquo;s computing power has grown a hundred-trillion-fold since 2010. Fitted the way the price is, it follows a power law over any stretch you pick, but a different one each time: the growth keeps slowing, and the price moves first.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="hr-attribution">
    <div class="hr-attribution-label">Lineage &amp; attribution</div>
    <p><strong>Dr.&nbsp;Giovanni Santostasi</strong>, who first framed bitcoin&rsquo;s price as a power law of time, has fitted the hash rate and active addresses the same way. <strong>Stephen Perrenod</strong> fitted the mining difficulty, which tracks the hash rate, and found an index of about 11.4 (<a href="https://stephenperrenod.substack.com/p/bitcoins-power-laws" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;Bitcoin&rsquo;s power laws&rdquo;</a>). The fits below, the rolling exponent and the price comparisons are this site&rsquo;s own, computed from Blockchain.com&rsquo;s hash-rate estimates and the site&rsquo;s price record.</p>
  </div>

