  <section class="hr-section">
    <h2>What the hash rate is</h2>
    <p>Miners compete to add the next block by running a calculation, a hash, over and over until one comes out below a target. The hash rate is how many of those guesses the whole network makes each second. Nobody reports it: it is estimated from how hard the target is (the difficulty) and how fast blocks are actually found, so a single day&rsquo;s figure is noisy. This page uses weekly averages.</p>
    <p>It matters because it is the cost of attacking the network. To rewrite recent blocks, an attacker would need more computing power than everyone else combined, and every rise in the hash rate raises that bar. Bitcoin&rsquo;s security is bought with this work, and the work is paid for by the block reward and fees, which is to say, by the price.</p>
  </section>

