  <section class="hr-section">
    <p class="hr-read" id="hrLagRead"></p>
    <p>That order is what the economics would predict. A higher price raises what each block pays, miners buy and plug in more machines, and the new machines take months to order, ship and power up. The hash rate is the security the price pays for, and it follows the price; it does not forecast it. A rising hash rate says the network is getting harder to attack, and that miners expect mining to pay. It says nothing about where the price goes next.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="hr-section hr-pointer">
    <h2>What this page does not show</h2>
    <p>A high hash rate is not the whole of security. How the mining is spread matters too: across pools, across countries, across power sources. <a href="/risks-to-bitcoin">Risks to Bitcoin</a> covers pool concentration and where the miners are. For bitcoin&rsquo;s price as a power law of time, the trend this page measures the hash rate against, start with <a href="/the-power-law">The Power Law</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="hr-caveats">
    <h3>Method and sources</h3>
    <p><strong>Hash rate:</strong> Blockchain.com&rsquo;s daily estimate (<a href="https://www.blockchain.com/explorer/charts/hash-rate" target="_blank" rel="noopener">chart</a>), averaged by week from 2010; today&rsquo;s figure is the mean of the last seven daily estimates. Other trackers estimate it differently and can differ by a few percent.</p>
    <p><strong>The fits:</strong> least squares of the logarithm of hash rate on the logarithm of days since the genesis block (3 January 2009), the same form as the price power law, from 2011, 2016 and 2020, and over rolling four-year windows, refitted each time the page loads. Doubling times come from the slope of the hash rate&rsquo;s logarithm against time over each window.</p>
    <p><strong>Price against hash rate:</strong> the site&rsquo;s price record (the samples behind <a href="/the-power-law">The Power Law</a>) against the hash rate of the same week. <strong>Which moves first:</strong> year-over-year changes in the logarithms of price and hash rate, monthly from 2014, correlated with one shifted by up to twelve months. A correlation shows the two move together at that shift; it does not by itself prove which causes which.</p>
    <p><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.blockchain.com/explorer/charts/hash-rate" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Blockchain.com, hash rate</a>; <a href="https://stephenperrenod.substack.com/p/bitcoins-power-laws" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stephen Perrenod, &ldquo;Bitcoin&rsquo;s power laws&rdquo;</a>; Giovanni Santostasi&rsquo;s power-law work on hash rate and addresses.</p>
  </div>

